1. vendor/certlane/ : matPTF (INGV), CC BY 4.0
----------------------------------------------

Files: vendor/certlane/primitives.py, vendor/certlane/test_primitives.py,
vendor/certlane/attack_primitives.py.

certlane is a port, into ball arithmetic, of the alert layer of matPTF, the
probabilistic tsunami forecasting code of INGV.

  Authors   J. Selva, S. Lorito, M. Volpe, F. Romano, R. Tonini, P. Perfetti,
            F. Bernardi and co-authors
  Code      https://github.com/INGV/matPTF, version 1.0, commit
            fe4dd958b18de0b5464e83246f8fa95588d96f43
            (archived as doi:10.5281/zenodo.6390936);
            files PTF_AlertLevels.m and PTF_1_0.m
  License   Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0),
            https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
            (the file LICENSE.txt of the matPTF repository). The matPTF source
            files carry no copyright line.
  Paper     Selva, J., Lorito, S., Volpe, M., Romano, F., Tonini, R.,
            Perfetti, P., Bernardi, F., Taroni, M., Scala, A., Babeyko, A.,
            Løvholt, F., Gibbons, S.J., Macías, J., Castro, M.J.,
            González-Vida, J.M., Sánchez-Linares, C., Bayraktar, H.B.,
            Basili, R., Maesano, F.E., Tiberti, M.M., Mele, F., Piatanesi, A.,
            Amato, A., "Probabilistic tsunami forecasting for early warning",
            Nat. Commun. 12, 5677 (2021),
            https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-021-25815-w

What was taken. primitives.py was written with the Matlab code open. Its
routines _interp_path, cert_percentile_bracket, _boundary_ball and
cert_alert_level, and the mu = -inf rule of cert_lognormal_survival, follow
PTF_AlertLevels.m (lines 26, 104-121, 139, 143) and PTF_1_0.m (lines 768,
776-785) step for step and reproduce their conventions. The functions
oracle_percentile and oracle_alert_level in test_primitives.py and in
attack_primitives.py are Python translations of PTF_AlertLevels.m, lines
106-121 and 139. These portions remain under CC BY 4.0.

Changes made. The material was translated from Matlab to Python. In
primitives.py the arithmetic is done in Arb balls instead of float64,
comparisons that the balls cannot decide are carried as a union over all
consistent paths, and the INDET and STRADDLES results are added. In the two
test files the oracles are evaluated with mpmath at 60 and 80 digits, and
explicit returns are added for degenerate inputs (a curve that is not
monotone, an index past the last threshold, a probability out of range). The
enclosures, the handling of undecidable comparisons, the convex-combination
tightener (cert_normalized_weighted_sum), the containment tests, the
planted-error controls and the attack harness are additions by BootLoops,
under the MIT License.

No endorsement. INGV and the authors of matPTF do not endorse this port.
certlane is a research instrument for studying the
numerical certification of a published hazard computation. It is not a
tsunami-warning system and must not be used operationally. The matPTF
material is provided under the disclaimer of warranties and limitation of
liability of Section 5 of the CC BY 4.0 license.


2. vendor/geo/mc.py : pyCSEP, BSD-3-Clause
------------------------------------------

mc.py follows the Poisson L-test simulation of pyCSEP.

  Authors   W. H. Savran, M. J. Werner, D. Schorlemmer, P. J. Maechling and
            co-authors
  Code      https://github.com/cseptesting/pycsep; functions
            _simulate_catalog and _poisson_likelihood_test
            (csep/core/poisson_evaluations.py) and
            poisson_joint_log_likelihood_ndarray (csep/utils/stats.py)
  Paper     W. H. Savran, M. J. Werner, D. Schorlemmer, P. J. Maechling,
            "pyCSEP: A Python Toolkit For Earthquake Forecast
            Developers", J. Open Source Softw. 7(69) (2022) 3658

What was taken. mc.py was written with that code open. The sampling statement
numpy.searchsorted(..., side='right') in sim_ll_fixed_n and sim_ll_poisson_n
is pyCSEP's, the two simulation functions follow the loop of
_poisson_likelihood_test, and obs_ll keeps the three terms of pyCSEP's joint
log-likelihood in the same order. The pyCSEP version that was open when
mc.py was written was not recorded; the comparison on record was made against
commit 2f9f66bf130eca99c7b0205f9919f0685075842f.

Changes made. The simulation is batched over many catalogs at once, the
multiplicity penalty is computed from sorted runs, catalogs are grouped by
event count, and a Clopper-Pearson interval (quantile_ci) is added. These
additions are by BootLoops, under the MIT License.

